WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.091
Valid JSON, invented facts.

2
00:00:02.371 --> 00:00:06.637
By the end of lesson 8 your extractor can’t produce malformed output.

3
00:00:06.917 --> 00:00:08.709
Now look at what it can still produce.

4
00:00:08.989 --> 00:00:15.091
Picture a transcript where Sam says “I can update the roadmap slide, but I need the final numbers first.

5
00:00:15.371 --> 00:00:24.544
” A schema-valid extraction might read: task “Update the roadmap slide”, owner “Sam”, due date 2026-11-14.

6
00:00:24.824 --> 00:00:26.189
The owner is plausible.

7
00:00:26.469 --> 00:00:28.048
The due date appears nowhere.

8
00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:33.427
Nothing in the JSON looks different from a correct item, and the date is formatted perfectly.

9
00:00:33.707 --> 00:00:36.480
This is the central trust problem of extraction.

10
00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.960
The schema guarantees shape; it doesn’t make values true.

11
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:53.211
Google’s Gemini docs warn about schema-compliant but semantically incorrect outputs, and Anthropic’s own guidance on hallucinations says that even with good techniques you should validate critical information.

12
00:00:53.491 --> 00:01:01.405
Constrained decoding can also add pressure to invent: if a field is required and non-nullable, the model must write something.

13
00:01:01.685 --> 00:01:04.245
The studio project grades you on this directly.

14
00:01:04.525 --> 00:01:07.000
A list of action items is easy to produce.

15
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:15.301
A list where every item can be traced to what was actually said, with doubtful items flagged instead of silently kept, is the deliverable.

16
00:01:15.581 --> 00:01:23.304
This lesson builds the checks, from cheap and certain to expensive and fallible, and ends with how to decide what a person should look at.
